    purpose:
        'Vehicle and road traffic incident rescue is the coordinated response to a collision, rollover, struck-by event, vehicle fire, pedestrian impact, or person trapped in or beneath a vehicle. The priorities are scene safety, early alarm, prevention of secondary collisions, rapid access to professional medical care, and controlled casualty movement. Untrained personnel must not attempt technical vehicle extrication or enter an unstable traffic scene.',
    scope:
        'Applies to construction-site roads, public-road interfaces, haul routes, logistics yards, parking areas, reversing zones, buses, light vehicles, forklifts, dump trucks, mobile plant and transport operations. Adapt the response to traffic speed, road geometry, visibility, load stability, vehicle energy sources, weather, nearby utilities, and access for ambulance/fire-rescue services.',
    keyKnowledge: <String>[
      'Recognize incident types: vehicle-to-vehicle collision, vehicle-pedestrian impact, rollover, run-over, reversing incident, load shift, vehicle fire, fuel or battery release, and entrapment. More than one hazard may exist at the same time.',
      'The first arriving person should stop at a safe location, assess from a protected position, raise the alarm, provide exact location and access gate, and request the appropriate site emergency team and public emergency services through the approved contact route.',
      'Prevent secondary impact. Establish an exclusion zone using the site traffic emergency plan, safe upstream warning, traffic diversion, spotters and physical barriers where competent responders can place them safely. Do not stand in a live lane to direct traffic.',
      'Treat every involved vehicle as potentially unstable until assessed. Consider rolling, movement from damaged brakes, suspended loads, leaking fluids, damaged tyres, high-voltage hybrid/EV systems, airbags, pretensioners, compressed gas cylinders and stored energy.',
      'Do not move a casualty unless there is an immediate threat such as fire, explosion, or another danger that cannot be controlled, and movement can be performed without creating greater risk. Follow dispatcher, medic and rescue-team direction.',
      'Do not pull a trapped person, cut a seat belt without checking its load and casualty position, or operate damaged vehicle controls. Technical stabilization, glass management, cutting, spreading and lifting belong to trained rescue personnel using approved tools and methods.',
      'For suspected spinal injury, maintain the casualty in the position found when safe, support the head/neck without force, monitor breathing and responsiveness, control severe external bleeding within training, and avoid unnecessary movement.',
      'For vehicle fire, smoke, fuel leak, battery thermal event or hazardous cargo, withdraw, isolate the area, prevent ignition sources only when safe, and let trained fire/hazmat responders select tactics. Never approach a smoking or hissing high-voltage battery pack.',
      'Provide responders with vehicle type, number of casualties, entrapment status, fire/smoke, fuel or chemical release, traffic conditions, gate and route, and known hazards. Send a guide to meet responders without leaving the scene uncontrolled.',
      'Preserve evidence after life safety is addressed: protect the scene, record time and location, vehicle/equipment IDs, witnesses, photographs from safe positions, weather, traffic controls and initial actions. Do not delay rescue or medical care for investigation.',
      'After the event, suspend the affected route or activity until competent review confirms vehicle condition, road integrity, traffic controls, equipment inspection, operator authorization and corrective actions.',
      "Scene size-up should follow a deliberate sequence: safe approach, alarm, traffic control, casualty count, vehicle stability, fire/spill/electrical hazards, responder access, and a clear command handover. Reassess whenever traffic, weather, vehicle position or casualty condition changes.",
