  EmergencyRescueTopic(
    id: 'ER-14',
    title: 'Machinery Entrapment & Industrial Equipment Rescue',
    purpose:
        'Machinery entrapment rescue addresses a person caught, crushed, drawn-in, pinned or trapped by moving or stored-energy equipment. The response must prevent further movement, isolate hazardous energy through an authorized process, obtain medical and technical rescue support, and release the casualty in a controlled manner. Improvised reversing, cutting, lifting or restarting can worsen injury or cause a second casualty.',
    scope:
        'Applies to conveyors, rollers, presses, mixers, crushers, cutting machines, workshop equipment, forklifts, mobile plant, rotating shafts, powered gates, hydraulic systems, pneumatic systems and automated production lines. The actual method depends on machine design, energy sources, load position, guarding, manufacturer instructions and competent rescue capability.',
    keyKnowledge: <String>[
      'Identify energy sources: electrical supply, batteries, hydraulic pressure, pneumatic pressure, gravity, springs, flywheels, rotating inertia, counterweights, suspended loads, thermal energy and process materials.',
      'Emergency stop is not necessarily energy isolation. Stop controls may halt motion but leave stored energy, automatic restart capability or hazardous voltage. Apply the site energy-control/LOTO process by authorized personnel.',
      'Never reverse, jog, reset, cycle, lift or lower equipment to free a casualty unless a competent rescue lead has assessed the machine and approved the action under a controlled plan.',
      'Prevent unauthorized restart: control the operating station, isolate and lock/tag as required, verify zero-energy state using the approved method, and account for all energy sources before intervention.',
      'Stored pressure must be relieved or mechanically restrained only by trained personnel using manufacturer-approved procedures. A hydraulic ram, suspended component or spring can move suddenly when pressure changes.',
      'Do not remove guards, cut machine frames, dismantle components or use a forklift/crane to pull a trapped person without engineering and rescue-team assessment. These actions can shift load paths or create crushing hazards.',
      'Assess casualty condition and entrapment mechanism: compression, crush injury, bleeding, amputation, burns, electrical contact, chemical exposure or restricted breathing. Request ambulance and specialist rescue early.',
      'Do not release prolonged compression without medical/rescue coordination. The casualty may need controlled release and immediate advanced medical support; first aiders must not delay emergency services while attempting complex treatment.',
      'If a limb is trapped, do not force it free or apply improvised traction. Protect from further movement, control visible bleeding within training, maintain communication and prepare for the responder’s release plan.',
      'Use a clear command structure: incident lead, machine/energy isolation authority, technical rescue lead, first aider/medic, perimeter controller and communications liaison. One person coordinates instructions.',
      'After rescue, retain isolation until the machine is inspected, damaged parts are secured, guards/interlocks are restored, cause is investigated and formal authorization is given to return to service.',
      "Apply a machine-specific energy inventory: normal supply, alternative feeds, control circuits, batteries, gravity, pressure accumulators, trapped product, thermal energy and automatic sequences. A single isolator may not control every source.",
      "A zero-energy verification must use the approved method and suitable test equipment where applicable. Test-before-touch and electrical verification are for authorized competent personnel; controls must be returned to a safe state after verification.",
      "Group lockout or complex multi-team work requires a clear lock ownership and handover system. No one should remove another person's personal lock except through the formally approved exceptional process.",
      "If a casualty is pinned by a load, assess whether supporting members or the machine frame could shift when the load is relieved. Use engineered stabilization and controlled release; never place hands or body parts in a potential crush path.",
      "A rescue plan should identify who can authorize isolation, who understands the machine, who leads technical release, who provides medical care, and what backup exists if the planned release fails.",
      "Do not assume a machine is safe because its motor is silent. Gravity, stored pressure, rotating inertia, spring force or an automatic restart signal can remain hazardous after shutdown.",
      "Where amputation or severe bleeding is suspected, activate EMS immediately. Preserve any separated part only according to current first-aid/medical guidance; do not delay casualty care or improvise treatment.",
      "Machine guarding and interlocks are safety-critical. Temporary bypasses, defeated sensors, missing guards or unapproved modifications must be treated as serious restart barriers.",
    ],
    siteImplementation: <String>[
      'Pre-job: identify machines with entrapment potential, emergency stops, isolation points, stored-energy hazards, rescue access, competent authorized isolators and emergency contact arrangements.',
      'Ensure machine-specific isolation diagrams and manufacturer instructions are accessible to authorized personnel; do not rely on memory or generic lockout labels alone.',
      'Train operators to stop safely, raise alarm, keep others clear and never restart or reverse a machine to free a person without direction.',
      'At the incident: activate alarm; stop work in the surrounding area; prevent access to controls; appoint incident lead; request ambulance and technical rescue; identify machine and casualty status.',
      'Authorized personnel isolate all energy sources under the approved procedure, apply personal/group locks or equivalent site controls, release or restrain stored energy safely, and verify isolation before rescue access.',
