  EmergencyRescueTopic(
    id: 'ER-15',
    title: 'Water, Flooding & Drowning Rescue',
    purpose:
        'Water rescue covers a person overboard, fall into water, flooding, drowning or near-drowning, and incidents near tanks, pits, canals, shorelines or temporary water bodies. The safest initial response is often reach or throw from a stable position, raise the alarm, and bring trained water-rescue and medical responders. An untrained rescuer must not enter water or become a second casualty.',
    scope:
        'Applies to marine/offshore interfaces, waterfront projects, drainage channels, excavation flooding, tanks, reservoirs, temporary ponds, docks, boats and work near open water. Consider current, tide, depth, visibility, water temperature, contamination, entrapment, access, weather, electrical hazards and rescue equipment.',
    keyKnowledge: <String>[
      'Recognize distress: unexpected immersion, inability to keep the airway clear, silent struggling, loss of response, missing person near water, or a person clinging to an edge or object.',
      'Raise alarm immediately and state exact water location, access route, number of people, last-seen point, current/tide or flow, known hazards and whether the casualty is visible.',
      'Use the hierarchy of rescue: reach with a suitable tool from a secure position; throw a lifebuoy/line or flotation aid; use a rescue craft or trained water-rescue team; in-water entry only by trained, equipped responders under an approved plan.',
      'Do not jump in, swim after the casualty, form an unprotected human chain, or tie a line around a rescuer. A panicked casualty may grab and submerge a rescuer.',
      'Never enter floodwater or a tank/pit where electrical contact, contamination, hidden openings, strong flow, entrapment or unknown depth may exist without specialist assessment and controls.',
      'For man-overboard, maintain continuous visual pointing if safe, throw flotation, activate the vessel/site alarm, record last-seen location and follow the approved vessel or site recovery plan. Do not lose sight while retrieving equipment.',
      'Rescue craft and lines introduce hazards: propellers, entanglement, line recoil, unstable banks, edge collapse, poor visibility and changing current. Only trained operators should deploy them.',
      'Once recovered to a safe place, assess responsiveness and normal breathing. If unresponsive and not breathing normally, activate emergency medical response and begin CPR/AED according to current training and dispatcher instructions.',
      'Provide rescue breaths and CPR in accordance with current first-aid certification and emergency-dispatch guidance; drowning is an oxygen-related emergency. Do not delay resuscitation for attempts to drain water from the lungs.',
      'Do not use abdominal thrusts or hang the casualty upside down to remove water. These actions delay effective ventilation and may cause harm.',
      'Remove wet clothing and prevent further heat loss when safe; monitor breathing and consciousness. All significant submersion or suspected drowning requires urgent medical assessment, even if the person appears to recover.',
      'Contaminated water may expose rescuers and casualty to sewage, chemicals, biological hazards or sharp debris. Use appropriate PPE and arrange decontamination/medical advice as directed.',
      'After recovery, secure the water edge, account for all personnel, preserve last-seen information, inspect barriers and access controls, and do not resume work until the responsible authority releases the area.',
      "Differentiate still water, flowing water, tidal water and confined water spaces. Flow can pin a person against an obstruction; a bank can collapse; visibility can hide cables, debris, holes or entrapment points.",
      "A throw line must be deployed by a trained person from a stable position, with attention to line entanglement, recoil, snagging and a safe retrieval direction. Do not wrap the line around a rescuer or secure it to an unverified anchor.",
      "A person who has disappeared beneath water should be reported with the last-seen point and time, not a guessed location. Preserve witness observations and direct trained responders to the area; do not conduct an unplanned search entry.",
      "For a recovered casualty, check responsiveness and normal breathing. If not breathing normally, start CPR/AED as trained and follow current dispatcher instructions; if breathing, maintain airway, monitor continuously and arrange urgent medical assessment.",
      "Do not delay CPR to remove wet clothing, search for water in the lungs, or perform abdominal thrusts to expel water. Prioritize effective resuscitation and protection from further exposure.",
      "Consider secondary hazards during recovery: contaminated water, hypothermia, electrical services, unstable edge, boat propellers, ropes, sharp debris and bystanders crowding the landing point.",
      "A water rescue team needs a backup plan, communications, suitable PPE/flotation, defined entry/exit points and a safe recovery location. A boat launch or edge approach must not block ambulance access.",
      "Near-drowning can lead to delayed respiratory complications. Even a casualty who becomes alert after recovery needs medical evaluation and monitoring according to medical direction.",
    ],
    siteImplementation: <String>[
      'Before work: identify open water, tanks, pits, channels, flood pathways and edge hazards; provide suitable barriers, lighting, warning signs, access controls and approved rescue equipment based on risk assessment.',
      'Confirm who raises the alarm, who calls external emergency services, who deploys flotation aids, who controls the edge, and how responders access the exact location.',
      'Inspect and maintain life rings, throw lines, rescue poles, ladders, boats, radios and personal flotation equipment according to manufacturer and site procedures; keep equipment accessible and unobstructed.',
      'Brief workers on no-entry rescue, alarm words, last-seen-point reporting, safe throw/reach technique within training, and restrictions on entering water or flood zones.',
      'At incident: raise alarm; maintain visual contact and point to the casualty if safe; throw flotation/line from stable ground; call trained water-rescue and medical support; prevent others entering.',
