  EmergencyRescueTopic(
    id: 'ER-16',
    title: 'Heat Illness & Environmental Exposure Emergency',
    purpose:
        'Heat illness and environmental exposure response protects workers affected by heat strain, heat exhaustion, suspected heat stroke, dehydration, severe sun exposure, cold stress where relevant, or sudden weather-related deterioration. The response is to stop exposure, summon medical help early for serious symptoms, begin safe cooling or protection within training, and prevent recurrence. Suspected heat stroke is a life-threatening medical emergency.',
    scope:
        'Applies to outdoor construction, roadworks, logistics, utilities, industrial work, confined or poorly ventilated hot areas, vehicle cabins, remote work fronts and other tasks with heat or environmental exposure. Follow current UAE and Emirate requirements, employer heat-stress program, occupational-health advice, weather alerts and site-specific controls; legal dates, thresholds and work-rest rules must be verified from current official sources.',
    keyKnowledge: <String>[
      'Heat-related illness can progress from heat cramps or heat exhaustion to heat stroke. Warning signs may include unusual weakness, headache, dizziness, nausea, heavy sweating, cramps, confusion, collapse, seizure or altered consciousness.',
      'Suspected heat stroke may present with confusion, collapse, seizure or altered mental status in a hot environment. Do not wait for a temperature reading or assume sweating rules it out; call emergency medical services immediately.',
      'Stop work and move the affected person to a cooler, shaded or air-conditioned safe area if this can be done without delay or danger. Remove unnecessary outer clothing and start active cooling according to first-aid training and medical-dispatch guidance.',
      'For suspected heat stroke, rapid cooling is critical. Use available safe cooling methods appropriate to the setting and responder training; protect airway and continuously monitor breathing and responsiveness. Do not give oral fluids to a confused, unconscious, vomiting or poorly responsive person.',
      'For a fully alert person with mild heat symptoms who can swallow safely, provide cool drinking water or an appropriate oral rehydration drink in small amounts, rest and medical review as indicated by site protocol. Do not force fluids.',
      'Never send a symptomatic worker back to work simply because they feel temporarily better. Return-to-work decisions require medical/occupational-health guidance and review of contributing controls.',
      'Risk increases with high heat/humidity, radiant heat, heavy physical work, impermeable PPE, poor acclimatization, inadequate recovery, dehydration, illness and certain medications. Do not make assumptions about an individual’s fitness.',
